---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ViewportGenerator from '../../components/ViewportGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Viewport Meta Tag Generator" description="Generate a mobile viewport meta tag with custom scale and zoom settings.">
  <ViewportGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why a missing viewport tag breaks mobile rendering</h2>
  <p>Without a viewport meta tag, mobile browsers render pages at a desktop-width virtual viewport and then scale it down — forcing users to pinch-zoom just to read text, even on a well-designed responsive site.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Set the width (typically device-width) and initial scale.</li>
    <li>Decide whether to allow user zooming.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated meta tag into your page's head.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Omitting the viewport tag entirely</strong>, causing mobile browsers to render at desktop width regardless of your responsive CSS.</li>
    <li><strong>Disabling user zoom (user-scalable=no)</strong>, which hurts accessibility for users with low vision who rely on pinch-zoom to read content comfortably.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ensuring a new page renders correctly and responsively on mobile devices</li>
    <li>Fixing a page that appears zoomed out or requires manual zooming on mobile</li>
    <li>Reviewing accessibility implications of viewport zoom settings</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Should I ever disable zooming?</strong> Generally no — disabling zoom hurts accessibility for users with visual impairments; avoid it unless there's a very specific, well-justified reason for your particular application.</p>
</ToolLayout>
